\section*{Joint SLE and CLE limits at FK weights from one through four}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The linked manuscripts present the following claims by OpenAI: The later interface theorem extends the square-lattice FK claim through $q=4$, with the same SLE parameter relation and a separate complete nested-loop limit; For $1\leq q<4$, critical square-lattice FK interfaces are claimed to converge as curves to the predicted SLE, with a separate convergence theorem for the complete nested plane CLE collection. Together they provide the following support within the scopes described below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
The tiled comparison and angular-transfer method is supplemented by endpoint estimates at $q=4$. Boundary current tests identify the driving process, while full exploration and localization retain macroscopic loops; at $q=1$ this also yields the bond-percolation crossing formula.

Positive tiled laws and short-distance comparisons localize the interface in irregular disks. Angular transfer modes and a four-boundary-change current observable identify the Loewner driver; exploration iteration then matches the nested loops, retaining their multiplicities and traversal structure.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The theorem gives the Dobrushin interface in bounded marked Jordan domains and, separately, the nested CLE under the free whole-plane law. It does not give their joint decorated marked-domain limit requested here. The source stops at q<4, and its two limits are a marked-domain interface and separate free whole-plane loop collection rather than the requested joint marked-domain decoration. This entry records partial support; complete coverage of the question is not established by this catalogue.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 223 has no Lean scope page in this release. Its selected result is not presented as formalized. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

